::ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A school fundraiser has a $120 setup cost and earns $8 per item sold. Which expression represents profit after x items?
{
=A. 8x-120#Correct. Profit is revenue minus the fixed setup cost.
~B. 120x+8#This treats the setup cost as a per-item rate.
~C. 8x+120#This gives total revenue plus cost, not profit.
~D. 120-8x#This subtracts earnings from the cost, reversing the profit relationship.
}

::ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A data model predicts 42.6 students will attend a workshop. What is the most reasonable interpretation?
{
~A. Exactly 42.6 students attend#People must be counted with whole numbers, so this is not realistic.
=B. About 43 students attend#Correct. The model output should be rounded to a reasonable whole-number count.
~C. About 426 students attend#This moves the decimal point and greatly changes the prediction.
~D. No students attend#The prediction is positive, so zero does not match the model.
}

::ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which choice best describes a useful model for a real-world situation?
{
~A. A model that ignores units#Units are essential for interpreting the result.
~B. A model that always gives whole numbers#Some contexts use decimals, such as money, measurements, or rates.
=C. A model that fits the context and gives interpretable results#Correct. A useful model must connect mathematics to the situation.
~D. A model that never needs checking#Every model should be checked against data, constraints, and assumptions.
}

::ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A population grows by 6% each year from an initial value of 500. Which model is appropriate?
{
~A. y\=500+0.06x#This is linear growth, not percent growth each year.
~B. y\=0.06(500)ˣ#This makes the initial amount part of the base instead of the starting value.
~C. y\=500(0.94)ˣ#This represents a 6% decrease, not growth.
=D. y\=500(1.06)ˣ#Correct. Growth by 6% uses a factor of 1.06 each year.
}

::ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L07_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Two models fit a data set. Model A has residuals mostly near 0, while Model B has residuals that form a clear curve. Which model is better supported?
{
=A. Model A, because residuals near 0 with no pattern suggest a better fit#Correct. Random residuals close to 0 support the model choice.
~B. Model B, because a curved residual pattern proves the model is exact#A residual pattern usually means the chosen model misses structure in the data.
~C. Model B, because larger residuals are always better#Large residuals indicate larger prediction errors.
~D. Neither model can ever be compared#Models can be compared using residuals, context, and prediction accuracy.
}

